.TH TMX 3
.SH NAME
tmx \- high resolution time conversion support
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f(CW#include <tmx.h>\fP
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I tmx
library supports conversion between
string date specifications and
nanosecond resolution
\f(CWTime_t\fP
clock values.
\f(CWTime_t\fP
is the number of nanoseconds since the epoch,
.BR "Jan 1 00:00:00 UTC 1970" ,
with leap seconds omitted; it is a 64-bit integral type if the
implementation supports one, otherwise it is an emulated type.
The
\f(CWtmxsec(\fP\fIt\fP\f(CW)\fP
and
\f(CWtmxnsec(\fP\fIt\fP\f(CW)\fP
macros extract the seconds and nanoseconds portions of
.IR t ,
and
\f(CWtmxsns(\fP\fIs\fP, \fIn\fP\f(CW)\fP
composes a
\f(CWTime_t\fP
from a seconds value
.I s
and a nanoseconds value
.IR n .
\f(CWTMX_NOW\fP
is the current time,
\f(CWTMX_NOTIME\fP
is a distinguished invalid time, and
\f(CWTMX_MAXTIME\fP
is the maximum representable time.
.PP
The routines are:
.TP
\f(CWTime_t tmxdate(const char* \fIdate\fP, char** \fIend\fP, Time_t \fInow\fP)\fP
Parses the date specification
.I date
and returns the equivalent
\f(CWTime_t\fP
value.
If
.I end
is not
\f(CWNULL\fP
then it is set to the position of the first unrecognized character in
.IR date .
.I now
provides default values for omitted components in
.IR date .
.TP
\f(CWTime_t tmxduration(const char* \fIspan\fP, char** \fIend\fP)\fP
Parses the duration specification
.I span
(an ISO 8601
.B P
duration or a number followed by a unit)
and returns the equivalent number of nanoseconds.
If
.I end
is not
\f(CWNULL\fP
then it is set to the position of the first unrecognized character in
.IR span .
.TP
\f(CWchar* tmxfmt(char *\fIrestrict buf\fP, size_t \fIlen\fP, const char *\fIrestrict format\fP, Time_t \fIt\fP)\fP
Formats the time
.I t
into the buffer
.I buf
with size
.I len
bytes according to the format specification
.IR format .
A pointer to the end of
.I buf
(i.e., the terminating
\f(CW'\e0'\fP)
is returned.
The format directives are the same as those of
\f(CWtmfmt\fP
described in
.BR tm (3),
with nanosecond resolution for the
.BR %N ,
.B %s
and
.B %S
fields.
\f(CWtmfmt()\fP
is a seconds-resolution wrapper around
\f(CWtmxfmt()\fP.
.TP
\f(CWTime_t tmxleap(Time_t \fIt\fP)\fP
Returns
.I t
with leap seconds adjusted for external routines that do not handle
leap seconds.
Adjustments are only done if the
\f(CWTM_ADJUST\fP
flag is set in
\f(CWtm_info.flags\fP
(see
.BR tm (3)).
.TP
\f(CWTm_t* tmxmake(Time_t \fIt\fP)\fP
Returns a pointer to the
\f(CWTm_t\fP
struct (see
.BR tm (3))
corresponding to the time
.IR t .
.TP
\f(CWTime_t tmxscan(const char* \fIdate\fP, char** \fIend\fP, const char* \fIformat\fP, char** \fIfmt\fP, Time_t \fInow\fP, long \fIflags\fP)\fP
Scans the date specification
.I date
using the
.BR strptime (3)-style
.I format
(or one of the formats in the
.I fmt
list) and returns the equivalent
\f(CWTime_t\fP
value, with
.I now
providing defaults for omitted components.
.TP
\f(CWint tmxsleep(Time_t \fIt\fP)\fP
Suspends execution for the time span
.IR t .
0 is returned on success, \-1 on error or interruption.
.TP
\f(CWTime_t tmxtime(Tm_t* \fItp\fP, int \fIwest\fP)\fP
Returns the
\f(CWTime_t\fP
value corresponding to the
\f(CWTm_t\fP
struct
.IR tp .
If
.I west
is
\f(CWTM_LOCALZONE\fP
then
.I tp
is relative to the local time zone,
otherwise
.I west
is the number of minutes west of
.B UTC .
.TP
\f(CWTm_t* tmxtm(Tm_t* \fItp\fP, Time_t \fIt\fP, Tm_zone_t* \fIzone\fP, const char \fInewzone\fP)\fP
Sets
.I tp
to the
\f(CWTm_t\fP
breakdown of
.I t
relative to the time zone
.IR zone ,
or the current reference time zone if
.I zone
is 0.
.I tp
is returned.
.TP
\f(CWTime_t tmxgettime(void)\fP
Returns the current time.
.TP
\f(CWint tmxsettime(Time_t \fIt\fP)\fP
Sets the system time to
.IR t .
The caller must have sufficient privilege.
0 is returned on success, \-1 on error.
.TP
\f(CWint tmxtouch(const char* \fIpath\fP, Time_t \fIat\fP, Time_t \fImt\fP, Time_t \fIct\fP, int \fIflags\fP)\fP
Sets the file
.I path
access time from
.IR at ,
modify time from
.IR mt ,
and change time from
.IR ct .
Unless
\f(CWPATH_TOUCH_VERBATIM\fP
is set in
.IR flags ,
a time value of 0 uses the current time and a time value of
\f(CWTMX_NOTIME\fP
retains the previous file value.
0 is returned on success, \-1 on error.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR tm (3),
.BR tv (3)
